    (0, None, '\'Chef, hurry up the order already!\' Kostya says "give" in front of "faster", which is how Russians say "come on". The little "already" adds the impatience. He is a bartender with a guest waiting, and the chef is dawdling.'),
    (1, None, '\'The guest at table five is waiting.\' Russians sit "behind" a table, so it is "behind the fifth little table". The tiny ending on "table" is how a restaurant table sounds. The numbered tables are the waiters\' shorthand.'),
