    (3, None, '\'Great, taking it to the dining room.\' Russian has one word for carrying in your hands and another for going on foot, and this is the first one. "To the hall" is the dining room. Kostya is already half out of the door.'),
    (4, None, '\'Wait, one second, wait for me here!\' "Stand!" is how Barinov says "stop". Then "wait me", with no "for", as always. It\'s an order in two parts. Kostya knows the chef is about to ask for something.'),
    (5, None, '\'Chef, I need to work right now.\' "To me it\'s necessary" is the casual "I have to". Kostya tries to leave politely, and the chef doesn\'t let him. What russian calls "work" is a long one for such a quick excuse.'),
    (6, None, '\'Kostya, tell me, how are your affairs?\' "Tell me" is a command from the word he used earlier. "How are your affairs" is the usual greeting, now with a motive behind it. Barinov is oiling the way.'),
